{"candidates":[{"claim":"Resumption is permitted if and only if the event framing protocol deterministically identifies trailing bytes from budget exhaustion as an incomplete, uncommitted fragment (a non-event), and is refused if such trailing bytes are indistinguishable from a corrupted complete event.","counterconditions":["The framing protocol uses length-prefixing alone without end-markers, making a partial event indistinguishable from a complete one and corrupting causal continuity upon resumption.","Budget exhaustion occurs between an event's data write and a subsequent mandatory commit record, leaving a structurally complete but logically uncommitted event that naive framing would incorrectly validate.","The resumption protocol attempts to overwrite or garbage-collect the trailing partial bytes in place, violating the physical append-only integrity of the log."],"mechanism":"Self-delimiting framing with checksums causes the resumption protocol to recognize trailing bytes lacking a valid end-marker as non-events. Since non-events lack event identity, they were never committed to the logical stream. This causal separation means ignoring them does not delete or modify a committed event, preserving physical append-only integrity (SCR_006), while causal continuity (SCR_007) is preserved by resuming from the last validly framed event.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","d1c3422e3d18993ac034bc5bbd3b90f90d3294847aca130493c4c8c6272330cb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8}],"simulation_proposals":[]}